cuments v4.0 origin and preserves exactly ONE textual "sonification" identifier (line 7553) — this is the only remaining case-insensitive `sonif` match in the entire file, matching plan §2.12 literal spec. (4) Phase A.4 — verification passed: `grep -c -i 'sonif'` returns 1, `grep 'et_sonify'` returns 0, all three legacy private helpers `_audio_hue_from_rgb` (line 6917), `_audio_hue_to_d` (6928), `_segment_row` (7067) remain untouched in Stage 21.5 per the no-removal rule, AST parse of the edited 8210-line script confirms it is valid Python 3 with 100 function definitions total. Script grew from 7989 → 8210 lines (+221 for Stage 21.6). All edits applied via surgical `str_replace` with exact multi-line matching — no `create_file` on the script, no removal of any existing function/variable/comment. Three Tools applied: Identification = P (audio subsystem text) / D (terminology + function names) / T (rename operation); Descriptor Gap = the textual half of the sonification→native-music closure (the data-flow half is Phase B); Subsumption = zero textual remainder outside the deliberate historical-context line. Plan document updated via surgical str_replace edits to §11 Approval gate and §12 Version history. Next action: Phase B.1. |
| v1.4 | 2026-04-09 | **§9 rule 6 strengthened — "a fallback is itself considered an error."** User directive: every feature and every function SHALL do exactly what it is specified to do, and if code reaches a fallback path that fallback is treated as an error condition that MUST be reported via `_et_error(..., fatal=False, fallback_msg=...)` or `_et_fallback(context, reason, fallback_msg)`. The user's stated reasoning: "I hate fallbacks unless it is done specifically for certain things, as the feature or function SHOULD and SHALL do exactly as it should." Rule 6 previously read "No silent failures. Every fallback, every allocation failure, every error must be logged explicitly." — which was correct about logging but did not explicitly frame a fallback as an error condition in its own right. The new rule 6 makes the framing explicit, keeps the `_et_error`/`_et_fallback` infrastructure as the required mechanism, and codifies a narrow exception for **structural branches** that are not fallbacks at all: platform-equivalent branches (`sys.platform == 'win32'` vs Linux/macOS), capability-equivalent first-class paths (GPU vs CPU, float32 vs float64 precision selection, PNG vs TIFF frame format, libx264/libx265/libsvtav1 codec selection at Archival tier). These are design-level branches where both sides are equally valid primary paths, not fallbacks, and do not require error logging. Everything else — CUDA kernel failure dropping to CPU, raw probe path returning None, ffmpeg optical-flow failure dropping to raw assembly, MP3 encode failure leaving only WAV, FLAC sidecar step failing — IS a fallback AND IS an error, logged with the specific reason and the specific fallback action. Implication for Phase B onwards: the Phase B probe pass must NOT silently produce empty probe traces when voice-pixel selection fails; it must raise or log via `_et_error` with fatal=False. The Phase C synthesis must NOT silently produce zero-amplitude voices when a probe trace is degenerate; it must log via `_et_fallback`. The Phase D ffmpeg fallback chain (`cmd` → `cmd_raw`) already uses the correct pattern in the existing code (see lines 7907-7917 of the original script) and will be preserved and extended to the new tier-driven command builders. No script edits are needed in this plan update — it is a convention clarification that governs how Phase B/C/D/E code must be written. Applied via surgical str_replace to §9 item 6 and §7 Document version line, with this §12 v1.4 row added. Next action: Phase B.1 (unchanged from v1.3). |
